course: dictionary
lesson: 967
title: Words in context: blueberry
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A blueberry is a small, round, dark blue berry that is sweet and good to eat. The context tells you what kind of word it is. «Blueberries hold the same kind of colour, which is why blueberry pancakes with too much baking soda get green streaks.» Pancakes and streaks are clues that blueberries are food you can cook with and eat.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. Blueberry can also mean the bush the fruit grows on. Look for the clue words around it to know which meaning is right. If the sentence talks about picking, eating or cooking, it means the fruit. If it talks about leaves, roots or a garden, it means the bush.
explain: «Picture muffins coming out of the oven with green blueberries.» Blueberries are not usually green, so something must have changed them. The clue is too much baking soda. Ask yourself: what would fit here? Then test your idea in the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
explain: Be careful with words that sound like they are scientific. «"Superfood" is a selling word, not a science word.» The sentence warns you that superfood is a word used to sell food, not a word with a clear scientific meaning. When a text uses a word in quotation marks, the writer may be showing that the word is not quite what it seems.
fact: A blueberry is a small, round, dark blue berry that is sweet and good to eat.
fact: The blueberry bush is the plant that blueberries grow on.
fact: Blueberries are a fruit, and fruit has seeds.
fact: Blueberries hold a colour substance, so too much baking soda in the batter can turn them greenish. A squeeze of lemon juice helps keep them purple.
fact: "Superfood" is a selling word, not a science word: blueberries are healthy, but no single food cures diseases.
exercise: «Picture muffins coming out of the oven with green blueberries.» Why are the blueberries green? | They were picked too early | They were frozen overnight | They are a different fruit, a green berry | *Too much baking soda changed their colour
exercise: «We walked past the blueberry bushes by the garden wall.» What does blueberry mean in this sentence? | A kind of blue cloud | Muffins baked in a tin | *The plant the berries grow on | Small round blue fruits to eat
exercise: A writer says blueberries are a "superfood". Why might the writer put superfood in quotation marks? | It means the fruit is very big | *It is a selling word, not a science word | It is a scientific name for a special fruit | It means the fruit can cure any illness
test: What is a blueberry? | A kind of bread roll | A type of cloud | A large green vegetable | *A small, round, dark blue berry that is sweet to eat
test: Which is the plural of blueberry? | blueberry's | blueberrys | blueberrie | *blueberries
exam: «A squeeze of lemon would have kept the blueberries purple.» Why would lemon help? | *It helps keep the colour from turning green | It stops them from being blue | It makes the muffins rise | It makes the blueberries sweeter
